        "heading": "Patronymics: son of, daughter of",
        "content": "<p>The usual way to tell two Viking Leifs apart was to name their fathers. A son added <em>-son</em> to his father's name and a daughter added <em>-dóttir</em>: Leif Eriksson, Freydis Eiríksdóttir. (The exact spelling of the father's name changes in Old Norse grammar; the generator keeps it simple by adding -sson and -sdóttir.)</p><p>Iceland still uses this system today, which is why an Icelandic father and daughter usually have different last names.</p>"
